    {
      "field_name": "other_symptoms_established",
      "value": "profuse diaphoresis; hyperthermia; arterial hypertension; tachycardia; respiratory failure; rhabdomyolysis; multiorgan dysfunction; myoclonic jerks",
      "quote": "profuse diaphoresis, hyperthermia (41 °C), arterial hypertension (210/110 mm Hg), and tachycardia (160 beats/min). Progressive involvement of the diaphragm led to respiratory failure; thus, mechanical ventilation was inevitable. Despite the immediate infusion of midazolam (45 mg/h) and the muscle relaxant atracurium (60 mg/h), the jerky spasms could not be adequately suppressed, resulting in rhabdomyolysis with an increase in serum creatine kinase levels (29,482 U/L). Within 1 day, his clinical condition deteriorated dramatically, and multiorgan dysfunction developed, including arterial hypotension, cardiac infarction and heart failure, renal failure, pneumonia, metabolic acidosis, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. The attacks of myoclonic jerks and painful spasms required repeated IV boluses of 0.5 mg clonazepam.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
